        <div class="top-content"><ul class="list-unstyled"><li><div class="card  "><div class="card-content"><div class="card-header card__header mb-2"><div class="d-flex align-items-center mb-2"><span class="overline card__meta text-uppercase"><a href="/topic/type/research-article" class="card__meta__type">Research Article</a><span class="pl-2 ml-2 pl-2 border-left border-darker-gray  card__meta__date">May 27, 2025</span><span title="Free Access" data-toggle="tooltip" data-placement="bottom" class="pl-2 ml-2 border-left border-darker-gray  card__meta__access meta__access meta__access--free"><i class="icon-ok"></i></span></span></div><h3 class="article-title card__title"><a href="/doi/10.1073/pnas.2502567122" title="A green dichromophoric protein enabling foliage mimicry in arthropods" class="text-reset animation-underline">A green dichromophoric protein enabling foliage mimicry in arthropods</a></h3></div><div class="card-body card__abstract"><div class="card-body card__abstract">Our research sheds light on the fascinating molecular mechanism behind the insect
   green camouflage, a mystery that has intrigued scientists for over a century. By isolating
   and identifying a unique green pigment from the bush-cricket, we found ...Molecular mechanisms underlying the green insect camouflage have puzzled researchers
   for over a century. Here, we isolated and identified a green water-soluble protein
   from the integument of bush-cricket <i>Tettigonia cantans</i>. De novo sequencing and cloning ...<i></i></div></div><div data-visible-items-sm="2" data-visible-items-md="2" data-visible-items="2" data-truncate-less="less" data-truncate-dots="true" class="card-contribs authors text-reset authors-truncate"><ul class="list-inline" title="list of authors">
